<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-overdamped" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Behavior of Overdamped Solutions</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: SOLUTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-overdamped-form">
    <title>Overdamped Solution Form</title>
    <p>
      When <m>c^2 > 4km</m>, the characteristic equation has two distinct real roots
      <m>r_1</m> and <m>r_2</m>, and the system is <term>overdamped</term>.
      Since <m>m, c, k > 0</m>, both roots are negative.
      The general solution takes the form:
      <me>x(t) = c_1 e^{r_1 t} + c_2 e^{r_2 t},</me>
      where <m>r_1 \lt 0</m> and <m>r_2 \lt 0</m>.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: SUM OF TWO DECAYING EXPONENTIALS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Unlike the underdamped case in <xref ref="sec-underdamped"/>, there are no
    trigonometric functions here. The solution is a sum of two purely exponentially
    decaying terms. This means position function is not oscillating. 
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 1: SPRING ANIMATION + x(t) GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-spring">
    <caption>
      An overdamped spring with <m>r_1 = -0.5</m>, <m>r_2 = -2</m>,
      <m>c_1 = -2</m>, <m>c_2 = 3</m>, so
      <m>x(t) = -2e^{-0.5t} + 3e^{-2t}</m>.
      Press Play and notice the mass crosses equilibrium once then decays
      back without oscillating.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-spring"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-overdamped-spring.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2.2">
      <description>
        On the left, an animation of an overdamped mass on a spring and dashpot.
        The mass starts compressed, shoots through the equilibrium position once
        with momentum, peaks on the stretched side, then decays back toward zero
        without oscillating. On the right, the corresponding graph of x of t is
        traced in real time.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: RAISE THE EQUILIBRIUM QUESTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the animation above the mass crossed equilibrium exactly once. But does that
    always happen, or does it depend on <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>?
    Explore the graph below by trying different values.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 2: EXPLORER (GRAPH ONLY, c1/c2 SLIDERS)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-overdamped-explore">
    <caption>
      The overdamped solution <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{-0.5t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m> with
      <m>r_1 = -0.5</m> and <m>r_2 = -2</m> fixed.
      Drag the sliders to change <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> and observe how many
      times the curve crosses <m>x = 0</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-overdamped-explore"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-overdamped-explore.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2.9">
      <description>
        A graph of the overdamped solution with sliders for c1 and c2.
        The graph updates immediately as sliders move and a message reports
        how many times the curve crosses x equals 0. Students discover that
        the curve crosses at most once regardless of c1 and c2.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-overdamped-crossings">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on your exploration above, what are the possibilities for how many
        times the mass passes through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m> in
        an overdamped system?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Never or exactly once.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The mass passes through equilibrium at most once. Depending on
            the values of <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, it may cross once or never
            cross at all. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Exactly once, always.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not always. Try <m>c_1 = 2</m> and <m>c_2 = 1</m> in the explorer above.
            The curve never crosses <m>x = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Infinitely many times.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is the underdamped case. In an overdamped system there is no
            oscillation, so the curve cannot cross equilibrium infinitely many times.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Exactly twice.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Try as many values of <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> as you like in the
            explorer. You will not find a case where the curve crosses twice.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: ALGEBRAIC PROOF OF AT MOST ONCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-overdamped-crossings-algebra">
    <title>At Most One Equilibrium Crossing</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the mass in an overdamped system passes through the equilibrium
        position at most one time.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We want to find all times <m>t \geq 0</m> where <m>x(t) = 0</m>.
        Setting the general solution equal to zero:
        <me>c_1 e^{r_1 t} + c_2 e^{r_2 t} = 0.</me>
        Since <m>e^{r_2 t} \neq 0</m> for all <m>t</m>, we can divide both sides by
        <m>e^{r_2 t}</m>:
        <me>c_1 e^{(r_1 - r_2)t} + c_2 = 0,</me>
        so
        <me>e^{(r_1 - r_2)t} = -\frac{c_2}{c_1}.</me>
        Taking the natural log of both sides (when the right side is positive):
        <me>t = \frac{1}{r_1 - r_2} \ln\!\left(-\frac{c_2}{c_1}\right).</me>
        This is at most one value of <m>t</m>. It gives a valid crossing only when
        <m>-c_2/c_1 > 0</m> and the resulting <m>t \geq 0</m>. Otherwise the mass
        never crosses equilibrium at all.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: LONG TERM BEHAVIOR
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-overdamped-limit">
    <p>
      Since both <m>r_1 \lt 0</m> and <m>r_2 \lt 0</m>, both exponential terms
      decay to zero:
      <me>
        \lim_{t \to \infty} x(t)
        = \lim_{t \to \infty}
          \Bigl(
            \underbrace{c_1 e^{r_1 t}}_{\to\, 0}
            +
            \underbrace{c_2 e^{r_2 t}}_{\to\, 0}
          \Bigr)
        = 0.
      </me>
      The mass always approaches equilibrium in the long run, crossing it at most once
      along the way.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Solution form:</alert> Overdamped solutions have the form
          <m>x(t) = c_1 e^{r_1 t} + c_2 e^{r_2 t}</m> where <m>r_1, r_2 \lt 0</m>
          are two distinct real roots.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>No oscillation:</alert> The solution is a sum of two decaying
          exponentials with no trigonometric part, so there is no oscillation.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Equilibrium crossings:</alert> The mass passes through <m>x = 0</m>
          at most once. Depending on <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, it may cross once
          or never.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Long-term behavior:</alert>
          <m>\displaystyle\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
